sec.p:
To quantify the cross-correlation between elongation rate ER(t) and flagellar activity A(t), we first subtracted the mean from each time series ER′(t) = ER(t) − ⟨ER⟩, A′(t) = A(t) − ⟨A⟩, and then used the function ‘correlate’ from the SciPy library in Python. The normalized cross-correlation is defined as
CorrER,A(lag)=∑tER′(t)A′(t+lag)(∑tER′(t)2)(∑tA′(t)2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{Corr}_{E R, A}(\operatorname{lag})=\frac{\sum_{t} E R^{\prime}(t) A^{\prime}(t+\operatorname{lag})}{\sqrt{\left(\sum_{t} E R^{\prime}(t)^{2}\right)\left(\sum_{t} A^{\prime}(t)^{2}\right)}}$$\end{document}
where l is the time lag. In practice, we evaluated the numerator using ‘correlate’ function from SciPy library in Python and normalized by the square root of the product of the zero-lag autocorrelations, by using the same function.

Single-cell simulations were implemented using rules for proteome allocation and division timing from ref. [14], allowing for analysis of instantaneous proteome allocation and growth rate at the level of the individual cell. For completeness, here we include a description of all relevant equations required to define the model, but for a detailed derivation and model justification, we refer readers to ref. [14]. Each individual cell can be described by a set of state variables which include its coarse-grained proteome allocation to five sectors of proteins grouped together based on function and defined by their mass fractions φi for i ∈ {R, P, X, Q, U}, specifically ribosomal (R), metabolic (P), division (X), housekeeping (Q), and exogenously expressed “unnecessary” (U) proteins. These sectors are coupled to the amino acid mass fraction of the cell, a. The ribosomal (ϕR) and amino acid (a) mass fractions together set the exponential growth rate, κ = d ln V /dt, given by:


κ=κt(a)ϕR−μns
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \kappa=\kappa_{t}(a) \phi_{R}-\mu_{n s}$$\end{document}



where μns is the non-specific degradation rate constant and κt(a)=κt0g(a)\begin{document}$\kappa_{t}(a)=\kappa_{t}^{0} g(a)$\end{document} is the translational efficiency, which is dependent on a such that translation becomes significantly attenuated at low amino acid levels (specifically g(a) = (a/at)2/[1 + (a/at)2]). The dynamics of each sector are given by:


dϕi/dt=κt(a)ϕR(fi(t)−ϕi)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{d} \phi_{i} / \mathrm{d} t=\kappa_{t}(a) \phi_{R}\left(f_{i}(t)-\phi_{i}\right)$$\end{document}



where fi(t) denotes the fraction of the total translational flux devoted to sector i. Both fR (t) and fX(t) obtain their time dependence through their dependence on other dynamic quantities, specifically a(t) and ϕRmax(t)\begin{document}$\phi_{R}^{\max }(t)$\end{document} These expressions are given by:


fx(a)=α(ϕRmax−fR(a))+β,fR(a,ϕRmax)=−f′(a)g(a)ϕRmax−f′(a)g(a)+f(a)g′(a)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered}f_{x}(a)=\alpha\left(\phi_{R}^{\max }-f_{R}(a)\right)+\beta, \\ f_{R}\left(a, \phi_{R}^{\max }\right)=\frac{-f^{\prime}(a) g(a) \phi_{R}^{\max }}{-f^{\prime}(a) g(a)+f(a) g^{\prime}(a)}\end{gathered}$$\end{document}



where f(a) = 1/[1 + (a/an)2], ϕRmax(t)\begin{document}$\phi_{R}^{\text {max }}(t)$\end{document} is a dynamic parameter constraining the maximum allocation to ribosomal proteins, and here y′(a) denotes the derivative of y with respect to a. fQ is taken to be invariant to nutrient changes, thus imposing the additional constraint fR+fP+fX=1−fQ=ϕmax=ϕRmax+β\begin{document}$f_{R}+f_{P}+f_{X}=1-f_{Q}=\phi^{\max }=\phi_{R}^{\max }+\beta$\end{document} The dynamics of fU are discussed below. The dynamics of φi are coupled to the dynamics of a, which are given by:


da/dt=κn(a,c)(ϕmax−ϕR−ϕX−ϕU)+μns−κt(a)ϕR
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{d} a / \mathrm{d} t=\kappa_{n}(a, c)\left(\phi^{\max }-\phi_{R}-\phi_{X}-\phi_{U}\right)+\mu_{n s}-\kappa_{t}(a) \phi_{R}$$\end{document}



where κn(a,c)=κn0(c)f(a)\begin{document}$\kappa_{n}(a, c)=\kappa_{n}^{0}(c) f(a)$\end{document} is the nutritional efficiency and c is the extracellular nutrient quality. Finally, a division event occurs when a sufficient number of X proteins have accumulated. The dynamics of X are given by:


dX~dt=γfX(a)(κ+μns)V−μXX~
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{\mathrm{d} \tilde{X}}{\mathrm{~d} t}=\gamma f_{X}(a)\left(\kappa+\mu_{n s}\right) V-\mu_{X} \tilde{X}$$\end{document}



where here we track the normalized quantity X̃ = X/X0such that a cell divides when X̃ = 1, μX denotes the division protein degradation rate, and γ is a conversion factor relating protein mass fraction to protein number (see ref. [14] for details). To match experiments, at each division event a single daughter cell was simulated which inherited the intracellular composition of the mother cell, maintaining the overall population size.

Previous experimental work has shown that the major driver of heterogeneity in ribosome concentration across single cells is not stochastic production, but rather unequal partitioning of ribosomes at division due to the formation of larger clusters [39]. Motivated by this, we modeled the redistribution of ribosomes at division through a stochastic differential equation in which perturbations to the maximum allocation to ribosomes only occurred at division times, td, then decayed slowly with a mean-reversion timescale τR ≫ 0. Specifically:
dϕRmax=1τR(ϕRmax―−ϕRmax)dt+ϕRmaxδ(t−td)ξ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{d} \phi_{R}^{\text {max }}=\frac{1}{\tau_{R}}\left(\overline{\phi_{R}^{\text {max }}}-\phi_{R}^{\text {max }}\right) \mathrm{d} t+\phi_{R}^{\text {max }} \delta\left(\mathrm{t}-\mathrm{t}_{\mathrm{d}}\right) \xi$$\end{document}
where ϕRmax\begin{document}$\phi_{R}^{\max }$\end{document} is the mean maximum allocation to ribosomes, δ(·) is the Dirac delta function, and ξd∼N(0,σR2)\begin{document}$\xi_{d} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \sigma_{R}^{2}\right)$\end{document} denotes random jump magnitudes drawn from a Gaussian distribution of mean 0 and variance σR2\begin{document}$\sigma_{R}^{2}$\end{document} In the absence of clustering, σR = 0.

In contrast, we assumed the main driver of heterogeneity in unnecessary protein allocation is due to stochastic gene expression. As such, we modeled noise in unnecessary protein expression by assuming it continuously fluctuated around its mean allocation value fU\begin{document}$f_{U}$\end{document} with timescale τU and with volatility coefficient σU, yielding:
dfU=1τ(fU―−fU)dt+σUfUdWt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{d} f_{U}=\frac{1}{\tau}\left(\overline{f_{U}}-f_{U}\right) \mathrm{d} t+\sigma_{U} f_{U} \mathrm{~d} W_{t}$$\end{document}
where Wt denotes a Wiener process. Assuming constant cellular density, protein mass fraction is proportional to protein concentration, allowing us to rewrite our previous definition of promoter activity as: AU(t)=ϕU(t)κ(t)+dϕU(t)dt\begin{document}$A_{U}(t)=\phi_{U}(t) \kappa(t)+\frac{d \phi_{U}(t)}{d t}$\end{document} where ϕdt(t) is the mass fraction of unnecessary proteins and κ(t) is the single-cell growth rate at time t. Using this expression, along with our definition of sector dynamics from above (dϕU(t)dt=κ(t)(fU(t)−ϕU(t)))\begin{document}$\left(\frac{d \phi_{U}(t)}{d t}=\kappa(t)\left(f_{U}(t)-\phi_{U}(t)\right)\right)$\end{document} the activity of unnecessary protein expression could then be calculated as:
AU(t)=fU(t)κ(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle A_{U}(t)=f_{U}(t) \kappa(t)$$\end{document}
To simulate, we numerically integrated the set of coupled stochastic differential equations detailed above for each cell. The code used to generate all simulation results are available at: https://github.com/josiah-k/Simpsons-paradox. The single-cell growth rate and corresponding activity for 3000 individual cells was computed at intervals of Δt = 3min for 75 h total.
